WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:02.240
Speaker 1: Let's see.

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:04.200
Where do we want to go next?

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.480
Let's try.

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:06.830
Let's go north of the border.

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:08.640
Let's go to Alex from Quebec.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.280
Speaker 3: Hi, David, can you hear me?

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:14.220
Speaker 1: Yes, I can be on venue.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:17.090
Speaker 3: Merci beaucoup.

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:19.490
I'm actually a first-time caller.

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:28.289
And before the pandemic in Quebec, the the
government had promised electoral reform away

00:00:28.289 --> 00:00:35.649
from first past the post and into mixed member
proportional representation system.

00:00:35.649 --> 00:00:36.649
Sure.

00:00:36.649 --> 00:00:41.800
But among the advocates of electoral reform,
there was a big debate between those who wanted

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:49.440
proportional representation and those who
preferred a preferential voting system.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:56.340
Justin Trudeau actually was more of an advocate
of the second option, while the provincial

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:59.340
government preferred the first option.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:03.370
And I was just wondering what was your preference
between the two?

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:11.259
Speaker 1: Well, I, you know, I always hesitate
to weigh in specifically about questions in

00:01:11.259 --> 00:01:17.979
front of other countries because I just don't
feel as if I'm as well-informed as I could

00:01:17.979 --> 00:01:20.899
be or should be really to be commenting.

00:01:20.899 --> 00:01:27.799
But I've talked over the years about a number
of different reforms to how we do our elections

00:01:27.799 --> 00:01:29.599
that I would welcome.

00:01:29.599 --> 00:01:34.290
I would love a proportional representation
system, big picture, because it could be a

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:37.920
way for third parties to make inroads in the
United States.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:43.289
The risk, of course, is you get five percent
white supremacists or something crazy, and

00:01:43.289 --> 00:01:45.279
that's something that that would have to be
dealt with.

00:01:45.279 --> 00:01:46.409
Then we would have to think about that.

00:01:46.409 --> 00:01:50.630
But I think proportional representation, especially
in the U.S., where this two party duopoly

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:53.869
is a complete and total mess, would be an
interesting thing.

00:01:53.869 --> 00:01:58.090
Switching away from first past the post to
something like single transferable vote or

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:02.880
one of the many different ranked choice voting
systems that don't have some of the problems

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.140
that we've identified in a few of the systems
would also be very much welcome.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.940
A combination of the two is something that
I would be open to.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:14.769
I welcome that, and in the U.S., I've talked
about a lot in the context of Canada.

00:02:14.769 --> 00:02:18.390
It seems more appropriate to ask someone who's
been following the Canadian discussion.

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:21.140
Speaker 3: Well.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:24.099
In my honest opinion, yes.

00:02:24.099 --> 00:02:29.560
Everyone just advocates for the reform that's
advantageous them generally, for example.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:30.560
Yeah.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:35.370
Justin Trudeau leads a centrist party, so
of course he will prefer a preferential voting

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:36.489
system.

00:02:36.489 --> 00:02:40.060
If he ends up against a right wing candidate,
he'll get the wing vote.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:44.799
And if he ends up against a left wing candidates,
he'll get the right wing vote.

00:02:44.799 --> 00:02:51.969
Whereas in Quebec, where the main issue has
been nationalist, not in the sense of like

00:02:51.969 --> 00:02:57.520
white nationalists in America, but nationalist
as wanting more autonomy for the province,

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:59.780
wanting to protect the French language and
so on.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:01.540
Of course, yeah.

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:08.249
Usually, these parties have split the votes
between left and the right wing nationalist,

00:03:08.249 --> 00:03:15.269
allowing for, well, parties who aren't that
to win more seats than they should have.

00:03:15.269 --> 00:03:21.049
And so Quebec nationalists, of course, prefer
a proportional voting system to increase their

00:03:21.049 --> 00:03:22.049
representation.

00:03:22.049 --> 00:03:27.310
Speaker 1: Yeah, on that on that front, what
what are the rules right now in terms of the

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:28.439
signage in Quebec?

00:03:28.439 --> 00:03:34.299
